WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.920
- Many of you in this
room know me and you know

00:00:01.920 --> 00:00:05.160
I pride myself on my work ethic.

00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:07.890
But buddy, I met my match.

00:00:07.890 --> 00:00:10.350
- [Commentator] Elon Musk
has chosen Linda Yaccarino,

00:00:10.350 --> 00:00:12.960
NBC Universal's former
head of advertising,

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:15.330
to become the new CEO of Twitter.

00:00:15.330 --> 00:00:17.820
Musk confirmed the
appointment on May 12th,

00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:22.230
a day after saying a new CEO
would start in about six weeks.

00:00:22.230 --> 00:00:24.630
Suzanne Vranica covers the advertising

00:00:24.630 --> 00:00:26.580
and marketing industries
for the "Journal."

00:00:26.580 --> 00:00:28.620
She explains Yaccarino's background

00:00:28.620 --> 00:00:30.030
and what her selection says

00:00:30.030 --> 00:00:32.230
about the direction
Twitter could be headed.

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:34.650
- Hi, my friend.

00:00:34.650 --> 00:00:37.920
- Linda Yaccarino is probably
the best known ad salesperson

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:39.240
there is on Madison Avenue.

00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:42.393
She's got a flamboyant
and outgoing personality.

00:00:43.380 --> 00:00:44.910
She's got relationships that go

00:00:44.910 --> 00:00:46.830
from Silicon Valley to New York.

00:00:46.830 --> 00:00:48.720
She knows marketers by first name.

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:51.630
She knows their kids and
she knows how to do her job.

00:00:51.630 --> 00:00:54.180
One media buying farm
calls Linda Yaccarino

00:00:54.180 --> 00:00:55.110
the Velvet Hammer.

00:00:55.110 --> 00:00:57.060
And the reason for that little nickname

00:00:57.060 --> 00:00:58.890
is because she is one tough cookie

00:00:58.890 --> 00:01:00.420
when it comes to getting money.

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:01.350
She can come off

00:01:01.350 --> 00:01:03.510
as the friendliest person
that you've ever known,

00:01:03.510 --> 00:01:07.020
but when they're in negotiations,
look out, she's tough.

00:01:07.020 --> 00:01:09.990
This is a real signal that
Twitter needs this person

00:01:09.990 --> 00:01:13.350
to reverse the advertising
slide that they've experienced.

00:01:13.350 --> 00:01:16.560
- You really need no introduction,

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:20.880
but in many ways today
is your introduction

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:22.980
to the advertising community.

00:01:22.980 --> 00:01:23.813
Right?
- Hello, hello.

00:01:23.813 --> 00:01:25.260
- I think that's a round of applause.

00:01:25.260 --> 00:01:26.640
- [Elon] Hello.

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:29.070
- [Suzanne] Miss Yaccarino
has been warming up to Elon

00:01:29.070 --> 00:01:31.830
for a couple months now and
she conducted an interview

00:01:31.830 --> 00:01:34.770
in Miami with him recently
and what was apparent

00:01:34.770 --> 00:01:36.930
from that is they both shared kind of

00:01:36.930 --> 00:01:39.300
the same vision for what
Twitter's going to be.

00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:42.510
- If freedom of speech, as he says,

00:01:42.510 --> 00:01:45.900
is the bedrock of of this country,

00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:47.400
I'm not sure there's anyone

00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:51.210
in this room who could disagree with that.

00:01:51.210 --> 00:01:53.490
Could I get a round of applause for that?

00:01:53.490 --> 00:01:56.100
- She very much believes
that it should be a place

00:01:56.100 --> 00:01:59.550
for free speech which is what
Elon has been talking about.

00:01:59.550 --> 00:02:01.620
He's very adamant and making sure

00:02:01.620 --> 00:02:03.240
that Twitter has different voices

00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:05.043
and opinions that can be heard.

00:02:05.970 --> 00:02:08.580
Following Elon Musk's takeover of Twitter,

00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:11.340
advertisers exited big time from Twitter.

00:02:11.340 --> 00:02:12.540
They were really afraid

00:02:12.540 --> 00:02:14.760
that Elon would lift all restrictions

00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:17.910
and their ads would be
appearing near unsafe content.

00:02:17.910 --> 00:02:19.458
That has basically kept a lot

00:02:19.458 --> 00:02:21.930
of them at bay and they have not returned.

00:02:21.930 --> 00:02:24.510
Linda Yaccarino knows full
well that this is an issue

00:02:24.510 --> 00:02:25.710
'cause she used to use this

00:02:25.710 --> 00:02:28.080
to her advantage when she was at NBCU.

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:30.060
She would attack the digital channels

00:02:30.060 --> 00:02:32.160
for having content that
was too controversial

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:34.290
and hoping to make sure
that brands would stick

00:02:34.290 --> 00:02:36.660
with big broadcast network for safety.

00:02:36.660 --> 00:02:39.810
So I suspect that she'll make
sure that Twitter invests

00:02:39.810 --> 00:02:43.230
in new tools to keep brands
away from unsafe content.

00:02:43.230 --> 00:02:44.310
The biggest challenge I think

00:02:44.310 --> 00:02:46.050
that Linda Yaccarino
will face when she heads

00:02:46.050 --> 00:02:47.820
to Twitter is Twitter is run

00:02:47.820 --> 00:02:49.650
by a bunch of engineers and coders

00:02:49.650 --> 00:02:53.100
and that's an experience that
she doesn't have much on.

00:02:53.100 --> 00:02:55.680
The good news is that
Elon has already signaled

00:02:55.680 --> 00:02:58.800
that he will handle the majority
of that part of business,

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:00.870
but it'll interesting enough to see how

00:03:00.870 --> 00:03:03.123
these two actually learn to work together.

00:03:06.363 --> 00:03:08.529
(light music)

